## Why This Is Asked

This question probes CRD design, per-namespace policy enforcement, drift detection, and the reconciliation loop. It tests translating high-level security baselines into concrete Kubernetes artifacts, and how to observe, audit, and test automated remediation in a multi-tenant environment.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes CRD design and controller reconciliation
- Translation of high-level baselines to NetworkPolicy and PodSecurity policy fragments
- Namespace-scoped overrides via labels
- Drift detection and automated remediation
- Observability: events, metrics, audit logs
- Testing: idempotency, end-to-end, rollback paths

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton reconcile function (pseudo)
async function reconcile(baseline) {
  const ns = baseline.metadata.namespace;
  const desiredPolicy = buildPoliciesFromBaseline(baseline.spec);
  patchNetworkPolicy(ns, desiredPolicy.network);
  patchPodSecurity(ns, desiredPolicy.podSecurity);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you instrument drift detection and alerting in a large fleet of namespaces?
- How do overrides get validated to prevent privilege escalation across tenants?
- What testing strategy would you use to validate namespace-scoped policies in CI and in a live cluster?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical Kubernetes-native policy enforcement, CRD design, and live migration safety. It probes how storage policy changes ripple through PVCs and how to keep observability.

## Key Concepts
- CustomResourceDefinition scoped to Namespace
- Reconciliation loop and safe PVC migration
- StorageClass pinning and encryption key references
- Observability via CRD status, events, and metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-reconciler sketch
function reconcile(dataPolicy){
  for (pvc of allPVsInNamespace){
    if (!dataPolicy.allowedSC.includes(pvc.storageClass))
      migratePVC(pvc, dataPolicy.allowedSC[0]);
  }
  updateStatus(dataPolicy);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement safe rollback if migration fails?
- How would you audit policy changes across namespaces and clusters?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses design of policy-driven mutating webhooks, dynamic CRD-backed configuration, and production-grade reliability (fail-closed, caching, hot reload, observability).

## Key Concepts

- MutatingAdmissionWebhook lifecycle, failure modes, and timeouts
- Policy CRD design with namespace overrides and defaults
- In-memory policy cache and Kubernetes watch-based invalidation
- Observability: metrics (latency, patch count), tracing, and audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
function applyPolicyToPod(pod, policy) {
  const ns = pod.metadata.namespace;
  const nsPolicy = policy[ns] || policy["default"];
  if (!nsPolicy) return pod;

  const containers = (pod.spec.containers || []).map((c) => {
    const req = c.resources?.requests || {};
    const lim = c.resources?.limits || {};
    const newReq = { ...req, cpu: nsPolicy.maxCPU, memory: nsPolicy.maxMemory };
    const newLim = { ...lim, cpu: nsPolicy.maxCPU, memory: nsPolicy.maxMemory };
    return { ...c, resources: { requests: newReq, limits: newLim } };
  });
  return { ...pod, spec: { ...pod.spec, containers } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you version policies and handle breaking changes?
- How would you test webhook fail-closed semantics under policy backend latency?
